# Quality & Consistency Checks

## Before Completing Any Component Build

Run this checklist and flag any issue to the user before marking a task done.

### Token Check

- [ ] Zero hardcoded hex values in component SCSS — colors via CSS custom properties (`var(--color-primary)`) or SCSS mirrors (`$primary`) declared in `src/styles/settings.scss`
- [ ] Zero raw spacing pixel values — use `var(--size-*)` / SCSS mirrors, or compose via `rem`/`em` scale
- [ ] Zero raw border-radius values — use shared radius tokens
- [ ] Zero raw breakpoint values in `@media` queries — use the `b-up` / `b-d` / `b-btw` / `b-o` mixins from `src/styles/mixins.scss` (they read the `$breakpoints` map)
- [ ] No `darken()` / `lighten()` / `rgba()` calls against `var(--...)` — color functions need SCSS values, so use the SCSS mirror (`$primary`) not the CSS variable
- [ ] Font sizes/families/weights via tokens (`--font-*`, `$font-*`) — never literal numbers in component SCSS

### State Completeness

Check if these states exist in the Figma design AND in code. Flag any that are missing from either.

- Default / rest
- Hover — all interactive elements (desktop only — gate with `@media (hover: hover)`)
- Focus / focus-visible — all interactive elements (keyboard reachable)
- Pressed / active — all interactive elements
- Disabled — interactive elements (`disabled` for native controls, `aria-disabled="true"` for custom)
- Loading / busy — elements that trigger async (`aria-busy="true"`)
- Error — inputs, forms, data displays
- Empty — lists, data displays
- Read-only — form fields where applicable

**Flag example**: "The Figma component has no error state for `AppInput`. Should I build it with a red border and error message below as the default, or add it to Figma first?"

### Merging Check

If two components being built are structurally identical with minor style differences, flag it:

"`AppComponentA` and `AppComponentB` appear to be the same component with different visual styles. Recommend merging into one component with a `variant` prop. Agree?"

### Multi-Theme Readiness

Project ships at least `summer` (default) and `winter` themes — and must be ready for more. Theme is selected via `<html data-theme="summer">` (see `src/styles/settings.scss`). Apply these rules in every component:

- All colors must resolve via CSS custom properties so the component automatically restyles when `data-theme` changes
- If a color literally cannot change between themes, it still goes through a named token — never hardcode
- Test rendered output under at least both existing themes before marking the task done

### Multi-Language Text Stress

For any component that contains text:

1. Does it handle text that is ~2× longer without breaking layout?
2. Does it handle text that wraps to 2 lines without breaking layout?

Apply unconditionally:

- No fixed `width` / `height` on text containers. Use `min-width` / `min-height` if a minimum is required.
- Allow wrapping unless the design explicitly says "single line only" — in which case use `white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;` and a tooltip if context is lost.
- Icon + label rows: label flexes, icon pinned with `flex-shrink: 0`.
- Avoid `position: absolute` for text labels — they overflow in long languages.

If not handled, raise: "The `App[Name]` label breaks with longer text. Should I (1) truncate with ellipsis, or (2) allow wrapping and adjust the layout?"

### Accessibility

- Prefer semantic HTML first (`<button>`, `<a>`, `<input>`, `<nav>`, `<dialog>`). ARIA only when no native element fits.
- All interactive elements reachable via keyboard. Visible focus indicator (no `outline: none` without a replacement).
- Buttons activate on Enter and Space. Links activate on Enter.
- `aria-label` on icon-only buttons. `aria-describedby` for error/help text on inputs. `aria-disabled`, `aria-busy`, `aria-selected`, `aria-expanded` where applicable.
- Decorative icons: `aria-hidden="true"` and no accessible name.
- Color contrast: minimum 4.5:1 for body text, 3:1 for large text and interactive boundaries — under every theme.

### Props Consistency

Props that appear in more than one component must use identical names and types:

```
disabled: boolean         (use native `disabled` for native controls; `aria-disabled` for custom widgets)
isLoading: boolean        (not: loading, pending, busy)
onClick: () => void       (not: onPress, onTap)
variant: string union     (not: type, kind, mode)
size: 'small' | 'medium' | 'large'   (use full names, not abbreviations)
className?: string        (always accepted on the root element)
```

If an existing component uses the wrong naming, flag it:

"I noticed `AppComponentX` uses `onTap` but the convention is `onClick`. Should I fix it now or log it as a TODO?"

### className Composition

`classnames` is the only allowed class-composition helper. See `14-classnames-cn-alias.mdc` for the full rule — quick recap:

```tsx
import cn from 'classnames'

<div className={cn(s.base, 'container', { [s.disabled]: disabled }, className)} />
```

No template literals. No `.join(' ')`. No ternaries for class toggles — use the object form.

